            <div xml:id="d711">

                <pb facs="0729" n="631" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg631"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">122.352/48: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Perkins</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-01-20T18:00:00-05:00">January 20, 1930—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>23. Your despatch 2462, December 5. The Department is inclined to consider
                    favorably the requests of the Chinese Government contained <pb facs="0730"
                        n="632" xml:id="pg632"/> in its note of September 23 last.<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d711-f1"/> However, the Department recognizes that disadvantages
                    might readily ensue from compliance with these requests and before coming to a
                    definite decision the Department desires that you endeavor to obtain additional
                    information regarding the views held and position likely to be taken by the
                    other Governments most concerned. You are authorized to intimate to your
                    colleagues in your discretion that this Government is not aware of convincing
                    objections from a technical standpoint that could be made to the Chinese
                    Government’s requests in this connection. Reply by telegraph.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cotton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
